<Project>

  <!--
    Product version stamping (#1804).

    The release workflows inject the tag-derived version explicitly:
      dotnet publish ... /p:Version=<tag, leading "v" and "+<build>" stripped>
    (.github/workflows/release-desktop.yml and deploy/Dockerfile.production's
    TASKDECK_VERSION build arg, fed by .github/workflows/release-container.yml).
    Build metadata is stripped because ProductVersion.Normalize drops it before
    reporting; stamping it would make the reported value un-comparable with what
    was injected.

    Any build that does NOT inject one — a developer machine, ordinary CI, a
    local `docker build` — falls back to a deliberately implausible 0.0.0-dev so
    an unstamped binary is recognisable instead of silently reporting a
    believable number. `Taskdeck.Application.Common.ProductVersion` reads the
    resulting AssemblyInformationalVersion at runtime and surfaces it on
    /health/live, /health/ready, and the CLI version command.

    A Release Desktop REHEARSAL dispatch is not an un-injected build: since
    #1795 `resolve-source` always yields a tag, so a rehearsal stamps that job's
    generated `0.0.0-dryrun` rather than this fallback. The fallback still covers
    every non-release build, including a rehearsal container build.

    This file sits at backend/ so every project in Taskdeck.sln — src and tests
    alike — is stamped from one place. Keep it in sync with the COPY line for it
    in deploy/Dockerfile.production, which needs it present before restore.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version Condition="'$(Version)' == ''">0.0.0-dev</Version>
    <Product>Taskdeck</Product>
    <Company>Taskdeck</Company>
    <!--
      Keep AssemblyInformationalVersion exactly equal to $(Version). Without this
      the SDK appends "+<SourceRevisionId>" whenever a build sets one, which would
      make the reported version drift from the release tag it was cut from.
    -->
    <IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>false</IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
